ui2/frontend/src/auth/adminApi.ts sends exactly this body and no role:

export function addDeviceSingle(
  address: string,
  vendor: Vendor,
  credentialReferenceId: string,
): Promise<AddDeviceSingleResult> {
  return call("/devices/add-single", "POST", {
    address,
    vendor,
    credential_reference_id: credentialReferenceId,
  });
}

The controller's request record is `record AddSingleRequest(@JsonProperty("address") String address, @JsonProperty("role") String role, @JsonProperty("vendor") String vendor, @JsonProperty("credential_reference_id") String credentialReferenceId)`, so the wire name is `role`.

DeviceRegistrationService.register refuses first, before the vendor check:

        if (role == null || (!role.equals("gateway") && !role.equals("management_server"))) {
            return new Outcome.ValidationFailed(REASON_ROLE_INVALID);
        }

with `public static final String REASON_ROLE_INVALID = "role_invalid";`. The two values are the whole vocabulary and the database enforces the same two.

The dialog is ui2/frontend/src/shell/AddDeviceDialog.tsx. It already holds `const [vendor, setVendor] = useState<Vendor>("check_point")` and filters the eligible credential list by vendor, so it has an existing pattern for a bounded choice that drives the rest of the form.
